  "version": 14,
  "description": "Independence rules. Everything is a { value, label } pair: value is the stable key used by logic and stored data and never changes; label is the text people see and can be renamed freely. Treatments and relationships are defined once below and rules refer to them by value. A relationship's phrase is the plain-language wording used in the independence decision. Each rule lists entries; each entry is one treatment, the relationships (measured from an audit client) it applies to, and exemptServiceLines: the service lines exempt from that treatment for those relationships (it can be empty). A relationship appears in at most one entry per rule, so use separate entries of the same treatment to give relationships different exemptions. The wizard embeds a copy of this file (without the prose fields). The target has a service line, chosen on the first page. When it is in an entry's exemptServiceLines, the result for that relationship becomes Exempt, the lowest priority treatment, so a restricted or materiality result from another audit client or rule still wins. Every other entity has no service line, which counts as not exempt.",

  "serviceLinesNote": "Service lines are { value, label } pairs. Values in exemptServiceLines are service line values such as TAX-101; a value ending in * is a prefix match, so TAX* is every TAX- service line. The treatments list is ordered strictest first, so exempt is last.",

